  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text'. Reply A is the text: '```json\\n{\"a\": 1} {\"b\": 2}\\n```\\n{\"c\": 3}'. Reply B is the text: '{\"c\": 3}'. For A, the rule requires the last object inside the fence, which is {\"b\": 2}. For B, with no fence, the rule requires the last top-level object, which is {\"c\": 3}. The check returns {\"c\": 3} for both, because in A the fenced content is not a single parseable object, so the fenced pool is skipped and the last top-level object is taken. I would be wrong if recover_json_object on A returned {\"b\": 2}, or if the two replies produced different answers."
